description:
Create a POSTURE rule. Posture rules evaluate compliance/security requirements against the cloud inventory (current resource state) — different from detection rules, which match log events. Use this when the requirement is "this resource should/shouldn't be configured this way" or "this connectivity path shouldn't exist".

Two rule types:

1) RESOURCE rule — matches a single resource configuration:
{
  "rule_type": "resource",
  "name": "S3 bucket must not be public",
  "description": "...",
  "severity": "CRITICAL"|"HIGH"|"MEDIUM"|"LOW",   // uppercase enum
  "category": "Security",                          // optional; default Security
  "state": "enabled",                              // optional; default enabled
  "labels": ["PCI", "CIS"],
  "resource_predicate": {
    "resource_type": "s3_bucket",
    "locations": [{ "location_type": "account", "location_value": "123456789012" }],  // optional
    "attributes": {
      "operand": "AND",
      "attributes_list": [
        { "name": "IsPublic", "value": ["true"], "match_type": "equals", "operand": "AND" }
      ]
    }
  }
}

2) PATH rule — matches connectivity between source/intermediate/destination:
{
  "rule_type": "path",
  "name": "EC2 must not reach Internet",
  "severity": "HIGH",
  "subject": "source",                             // which end the finding is attributed to
  "path_source_predicate": [{ "resource_type": "instance", "attributes": {...} }],
  "path_intermediate_predicate": [ ... ],         // optional
  "path_destination_predicate": [{ "resource_type": "Internet" }]
}

COMMON resource_types: s3_bucket, instance, network_interface, instance_volume, security_group, iam_role, iam_user, iam_policy, rds_instance, lambda_function, Internet (for the internet endpoint).

MATCH TYPES: equals, not_equals, contains, not_contains, is_null, is_not_null, greater_than, greater_equal, less_than, less_equal, matches_regex

EXAMPLE — public S3 bucket:
{"rule_type":"resource","name":"S3 bucket is public","severity":"HIGH","resource_predicate":{"resource_type":"s3_bucket","attributes":{"operand":"AND","attributes_list":[{"name":"IsPublic","value":["true"],"match_type":"equals","operand":"AND"}]}}}

EXAMPLE — EC2 → Internet path:
{"rule_type":"path","name":"EC2 to Internet","severity":"HIGH","subject":"source","path_source_predicate":[{"resource_type":"instance"}],"path_destination_predicate":[{"resource_type":"Internet"}]}

Create a detection rule. You MUST use the exact input structure below.

INPUT STRUCTURE:
{
  "name": "Rule Name",
  "description": "What this rule detects",
  "severity": "critical"|"high"|"medium"|"low",
  "labels": ["tag1", "tag2"],
  "custom_config": {
    "log_type": "identity"|"network"|"audit"|"process"|"file"|"apis",
    "detection_field": "source"|"destination"  // ONLY for network log_type
  },
  "condition": {
    "main_filter": {
      "operand": "and"|"or",
      "filters": [
        { "field": "<field_name>", "match_type": "<operator>", "value": "<value>" }
      ]
    }
  },
  "threshold_config": {  // OPTIONAL — for multi-event rules
    "time_window_sec": 600,
    "log_count": 5,
    "distinct_field": "src_ip",
    "distinct_count": 3
  }
}

FIELDS BY LOG TYPE:
- identity: action, src_ip, principal_id, principal_type, account_id, region, error_code, user_agent, session_id, is_mfa_authenticated, src_geo_iso. Use $.raw.* for CloudTrail requestParameters.
- network: src_ip_filter, dst_ip_filter, src_port_range, dst_port_range, protocol, src_vpc_id, dst_vpc_id, direction, dst_malicious_score
- audit: verb, principal_id, resource, namespace, response_code_range, object_name, api_group
- process: binary, parent_binary, arguments, function_names, event_type (process_exec|kprobe_syscall), pod, namespace
- file: binary, file_path, function_names (open|read|write|unlink|rename), pod, namespace
- apis: direction (inbound|outbound), http_method, http_host, http_path, http_status_code, user_agent, pod, namespace

MATCH TYPES: is, is_not, contains, not_contains, regex, gte, lte, exists, not_exists

EXAMPLE — detect S3 bucket deletion:
{"name":"S3 Bucket Deletion","severity":"critical","custom_config":{"log_type":"identity"},"condition":{"main_filter":{"operand":"and","filters":[{"field":"action","match_type":"contains","value":"DeleteBucket"}]}}}

EXAMPLE — detect traffic to malicious domain:
{"name":"C2 Domain Traffic","severity":"critical","custom_config":{"log_type":"apis"},"condition":{"main_filter":{"operand":"or","filters":[{"field":"http_host","match_type":"contains","value":"evil.com"}]}}}

Get available runtime agent actions for a specific resource. Call this FIRST to discover which actions can be taken and whether a runtime agent is connected.

Input: { resourceId: "<resource ARN or ID>" }
Output: { options: [{ action_type, display_name, description }], agent_connected: boolean }

If agent_connected is false, do NOT attempt to invoke — the action will expire without being picked up. Parameter names, types and per-action descriptions are NOT in this result: they are declared on agentActions__invoke's own `parameters` schema, so read them there.

SELECTING AN ACTION: Each option's "description" states what it does, "Use when:" the situations it is for, and "Do NOT use for:" when to choose something else. Pick the action whose "Use when" matches the actual threat in the detection AND whose required parameters you can fill from real evidence (a concrete malicious PID, file path, domain, or REMOTE peer IP — never the protected resource's own identifiers). If NO action's "Use when" fits (e.g. cloud-API or identity abuse, or data exfiltration with no malicious peer IP/PID/file, or a VM with no isolation primitive), do NOT force an ill-fitting action — emit a finding explaining why no runtime-agent action applies and recommend the response-action runbook path or manual escalation.
